# Drafted by Gemini (R11 batch b4), edited. joan_murdoch's first conversation.

~ start
set joan_murdoch_met
Narrator: Loose sheets smelling of printer's ink and lavender soap flutter in her hands. Her beaming smile fixes on you before you can step back.
joan_murdoch [smile]: Fresh faces, praise the song! Take a hymn-sheet, my husband Weston is inside tuning up the rafters!
-> menu

~ menu
joan_murdoch [smile]: Have you a moment to hear how the Morning Choir lifts the spirit?
* Ask about the hymn-sheets she is handing out -> work
* Ask how her family fares in Wickermoor -> village
* Ask what she hears from the townsfolk -> rumour
* [Persuasion DC 13] Gently ask why she presses the faith so hard -> secret_ok | secret_fail
* Take our leave -> END

~ work
joan_murdoch [smile]: I copy verses by candlelight while Peter and Charlotte practice harmonies. If folk hum along while churning butter, the light gets a foothold!
-> menu

~ village
joan_murdoch [smile]: Folk shut their shutters at first, shy as mice. Give me a month of knocking and every pew will be packed.
-> menu

~ rumour
joan_murdoch [weary]: Poor Mrs. Oakum at the butcher's bolted like a hare when I offered a pamphlet at dusk. Half this town acts like a kind word bites.
-> menu

~ secret_ok
Narrator: Her grin softens, and she smooths the songbook with ink-stained thumbs.
joan_murdoch [sad]: Without music, the gloom will swallow my children whole. I will knock on every door in the realm before I let the dark have my family.
-> menu

~ secret_fail
joan_murdoch [smile]: A good song is meant to be shared, dear heart! Keep that sheet dry and sing it when the road gets long.
-> menu
